WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.762
Type hints are another way to make code easier to understand.

00:00:03.762 --> 00:00:10.552
A type hint tells readers what kind of value a function expects and what kind of value it

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:12.077
returns.

00:00:12.077 --> 00:00:17.807
In this example, the average function expects a list of floating-point numbers and returns a floating-point

00:00:17.807 --> 00:00:21.902
number. Python will still run even if we pass a different kind of value,

00:00:21.902 --> 00:00:24.352
but the hint communicates our intent.

00:00:24.352 --> 00:00:28.967
This is especially helpful when functions are reused in larger projects.

00:00:28.967 --> 00:00:33.292
The function also checks whether the list is empty.

00:00:33.292 --> 00:00:35.427
If it is empty, it returns zero point zero.

00:00:35.427 --> 00:00:39.727
Otherwise, it returns the sum divided by the number of values.

00:00:39.727 --> 00:00:42.932
Run the cell and inspect the average score.

00:00:42.932 --> 00:00:46.427
Type hints are not required in every beginner script.

00:00:46.427 --> 00:00:51.952
But as projects grow, they make your code easier to read, test, and maintain.

00:00:51.952 --> 00:00:58.117
Think of type hints as part of the function contract: this function expects this kind of input and

00:00:58.117 --> 00:00:59.976
returns this kind of output.
